\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Physics is the label.} \textbf  {a)} Materials data fixes quantities that a stated claim must respect: which $2\theta $ can carry a reflection, through Bragg's law and the reference pattern of the named phase; the field of view, through the calibration a scale bar sets; whether a claimed spectral peak coincides with a genuine local maximum of sufficient prominence; and whether a composition lies within tolerance of the computed convex hull. Each constraint is checkable with no human annotation. \textbf  {b)} A chain is parsed into its checkable claims and each claim is tested against the oracle for its own constraint type, returning a per-claim verdict rather than a single score for the answer; detected violations are returned to the model by Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which is applied to the diffraction and spectra constraint types only (Methods). The chain and the values shown are schematic and illustrate the checks; they are not a model output. \textbf  {c)} The violations these oracles flag are legible physical mistakes, not parsing artifacts. \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  For the two oracle families whose human review postdates the final oracle definitions, an open-ended categorization of every confirmed violation }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}An open-ended categorization of every human-confirmed violation, shown here for the two image oracles that read a quantity off a plot or micrograph,} }\DIFaddendFL  gives a small taxonomy: diffraction failures are dominated by misreading the gridlines or the axis scale, and microscopy failures by scale-bar unit and unit-conversion errors, rather than by errors in the arithmetic that follows. Percentages are shares of the 78 and 93 confirmed violations respectively \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  (Methods)}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}(Methods); the same categorization for spectra and the three DFT families, on their final-oracle audits, is in Supplementary Table~S5}}\DIFaddendFL  .}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{5}{\justifying \textbf {Physics is the label.} \textbf {a)} Materials data fixes quantities that a stated claim must respect: which $2\theta $ can carry a reflection, through Bragg's law and the reference pattern of the named phase; the field of view, through the calibration a scale bar sets; whether a claimed spectral peak coincides with a genuine local maximum of sufficient prominence; and whether a composition lies within tolerance of the computed convex hull. Each constraint is checkable with no human annotation. \textbf {b)} A chain is parsed into its checkable claims and each claim is tested against the oracle for its own constraint type, returning a per-claim verdict rather than a single score for the answer; detected violations are returned to the model by Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which is applied to the diffraction and spectra constraint types only (Methods). The chain and the values shown are schematic and illustrate the checks; they are not a model output. \textbf {c)} The violations these oracles flag are legible physical mistakes, not parsing artifacts. \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {For the two oracle families whose human review postdates the final oracle definitions, an open-ended categorization of every confirmed violation }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {An open-ended categorization of every human-confirmed violation, shown here for the two image oracles that read a quantity off a plot or micrograph,} }\DIFaddendFL gives a small taxonomy: diffraction failures are dominated by misreading the gridlines or the axis scale, and microscopy failures by scale-bar unit and unit-conversion errors, rather than by errors in the arithmetic that follows. Percentages are shares of the 78 and 93 confirmed violations respectively \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {(Methods)}\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {(Methods); the same categorization for spectra and the three DFT families, on their final-oracle audits, is in Supplementary Table~S5}}\DIFaddendFL }{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  \textbf  {Oracle validity.} }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  \textbf  {Oracle validity and audited precision.} }\DIFaddendFL  The positive-control false-negative rate is the maximum across three planted violation rates (10\%, 30\%, and 50\%); all three rates yielded 0.0000 for every oracle family\DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}, which measures completeness on the injected violation types only}}\DIFaddendFL  . Audited precision is the fraction of oracle-fired cases from Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite outputs that human review confirmed as genuine violations of the stated constraint rather than parsing or oracle artifacts\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  . The spectrum result reflects the revised local-maximum-with-prominence oracle (Methods), evaluated over all 495 oracle-fired cases; the pre-revision result (72.8\%, $n=103$) is reported only for transparency. The three Materials Project-grounded results were obtained using the final operational definitions: near-hull classification, computed band-gap classification with an abstention band, and net-magnetization magnitude.}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}, with Wilson 95\% intervals; $n$ counts decided cases (2 spectrum cases marked unsure are excluded; counting them either way gives 92.3--92.7\%). Every audit was sampled and judged under the final oracle definitions used for all reported results (Methods): spectrum responses were collected once and the fired set re-scored with the final oracle before the audit, and DFT responses were re-collected under the final definitions before the audit; the diffraction and microscopy audits were carried out in July 2026 and the others in August 2026. Sources: NFFA, NFFA-EUROPE; MP, Materials Project. $^{a}$Final stability oracle with the near-hull abstention band: the 50 audited cases with $0<E_{\mathrm  {hull}}\le 0.05$ eV\,atom$^{-1}$ are not scored; under the original rule the audit gave 95.3\% [89.5, 98.0] ($n=107$). Consensus: a second annotator, blind to the first labels, re-labelled a random subsample of 30 audited cases per family stratified by the first annotator's verdict, and the two annotators adjudicated together every case on which they disagreed (24 in total); the consensus precision is weighted back to the audited population by stratum, with 95\% intervals from per-stratum Jeffreys posteriors (30 subsampled cases per family; $^{b}$14, the subsampled stability cases scored under the final oracle). Agreement statistics and the oracle-silent audit are in Supplementary Table~S8. The full audits were performed by one annotator (R.K.).}}\DIFaddendFL  }}{8}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:exp1}{{1}{8}{\justifying \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {\textbf {Oracle validity.} }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\textbf {Oracle validity and audited precision.} }\DIFaddendFL The positive-control false-negative rate is the maximum across three planted violation rates (10\%, 30\%, and 50\%); all three rates yielded 0.0000 for every oracle family\DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {, which measures completeness on the injected violation types only}}\DIFaddendFL . Audited precision is the fraction of oracle-fired cases from Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite outputs that human review confirmed as genuine violations of the stated constraint rather than parsing or oracle artifacts\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {. The spectrum result reflects the revised local-maximum-with-prominence oracle (Methods), evaluated over all 495 oracle-fired cases; the pre-revision result (72.8\%, $n=103$) is reported only for transparency. The three Materials Project-grounded results were obtained using the final operational definitions: near-hull classification, computed band-gap classification with an abstention band, and net-magnetization magnitude.}\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {, with Wilson 95\% intervals; $n$ counts decided cases (2 spectrum cases marked unsure are excluded; counting them either way gives 92.3--92.7\%). Every audit was sampled and judged under the final oracle definitions used for all reported results (Methods): spectrum responses were collected once and the fired set re-scored with the final oracle before the audit, and DFT responses were re-collected under the final definitions before the audit; the diffraction and microscopy audits were carried out in July 2026 and the others in August 2026. Sources: NFFA, NFFA-EUROPE; MP, Materials Project. $^{a}$Final stability oracle with the near-hull abstention band: the 50 audited cases with $0<E_{\mathrm {hull}}\le 0.05$ eV\,atom$^{-1}$ are not scored; under the original rule the audit gave 95.3\% [89.5, 98.0] ($n=107$). Consensus: a second annotator, blind to the first labels, re-labelled a random subsample of 30 audited cases per family stratified by the first annotator's verdict, and the two annotators adjudicated together every case on which they disagreed (24 in total); the consensus precision is weighted back to the audited population by stratum, with 95\% intervals from per-stratum Jeffreys posteriors (30 subsampled cases per family; $^{b}$14, the subsampled stability cases scored under the final oracle). Agreement statistics and the oracle-silent audit are in Supplementary Table~S8. The full audits were performed by one annotator (R.K.).}}\DIFaddendFL }{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Representative inconsistencies identified by the multimodal oracles.} \textbf  {a}, X-ray diffraction cases in which the model-reported $2\theta $ position lies outside the $0.5^\circ $ tolerance around the nearest genuine Bragg reflection. \textbf  {b}, Raman spectroscopy cases in which the model-reported Raman shift exceeds the $5\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$ tolerance relative to the nearest genuine spectral peak. \textbf  {c}, Microscopy cases in which the model-reported feature size is inconsistent with the physical scale indicated by the embedded scale bar, producing discrepancies of approximately $10$--$100\times $. All examples are human-confirmed oracle activations and illustrate representative tolerance, unit-conversion, and scale-factor failure modes.}}{9}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:oracle-examples}{{2}{9}{\justifying \textbf {Representative inconsistencies identified by the multimodal oracles.} \textbf {a}, X-ray diffraction cases in which the model-reported $2\theta $ position lies outside the $0.5^\circ $ tolerance around the nearest genuine Bragg reflection. \textbf {b}, Raman spectroscopy cases in which the model-reported Raman shift exceeds the $5\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$ tolerance relative to the nearest genuine spectral peak. \textbf {c}, Microscopy cases in which the model-reported feature size is inconsistent with the physical scale indicated by the embedded scale bar, producing discrepancies of approximately $10$--$100\times $. All examples are human-confirmed oracle activations and illustrate representative tolerance, unit-conversion, and scale-factor failure modes}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Main evaluation: Physical-Consistency Rate by constraint type and overall, \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  $n=30$ items }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}over the checkable responses among 30 attempted items} }\DIFaddendFL  per (model, constraint-type) cell per seed, $S=3$ seeds pooled per cell (bootstrap 95\% CIs omitted from this compact view; see Supplementary Table~S1 for the full per-cell confidence intervals \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}and checkable counts, Supplementary Table~S2 for coverage and alternative conventions, Supplementary Table~S3 for paired, Holm-corrected model comparisons, and Supplementary Table~S4 for class-balanced DFT rates}}\DIFaddendFL  , and the Methods for why $\rho _t=1-\mathrm  {PCR}$ here). DFT column combines the stability, metallicity, and magnitude constraint types\DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}; near-hull stability materials are not scored (Methods; the original rule's values are in Supplementary Table~S7)}}\DIFaddendFL  . The first four rows are the originally evaluated models; the next four are the later-generation refresh (Methods, Baselines subsection). $^{\ddagger }$GPT-5.6-Sol is OpenAI's flagship tier, added to test whether it exceeds the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  above }\DIFdelendFL  within the same generation; \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  its All-column bootstrap 95\% CI, $[0.731,0.806]$, overlaps Terra's $[0.736,0.810]$, so flagship does not separate from balanced despite leading on diffraction alone }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}the overall difference is $+0.002$ (95\% CI $[-0.042,0.046]$), not significant, so the design detects no difference between flagship and balanced tier larger than about 0.07} }\DIFaddendFL  (Discussion). \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}Bold marks the column maximum; it does not imply a significant difference from the next model.}}\DIFaddendFL  }}{12}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{2}{12}{\justifying Main evaluation: Physical-Consistency Rate by constraint type and overall, \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {$n=30$ items }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {over the checkable responses among 30 attempted items} }\DIFaddendFL per (model, constraint-type) cell per seed, $S=3$ seeds pooled per cell (bootstrap 95\% CIs omitted from this compact view; see Supplementary Table~S1 for the full per-cell confidence intervals \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {and checkable counts, Supplementary Table~S2 for coverage and alternative conventions, Supplementary Table~S3 for paired, Holm-corrected model comparisons, and Supplementary Table~S4 for class-balanced DFT rates}}\DIFaddendFL , and the Methods for why $\rho _t=1-\mathrm {PCR}$ here). DFT column combines the stability, metallicity, and magnitude constraint types\DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {; near-hull stability materials are not scored (Methods; the original rule's values are in Supplementary Table~S7)}}\DIFaddendFL . The first four rows are the originally evaluated models; the next four are the later-generation refresh (Methods, Baselines subsection). $^{\ddagger }$GPT-5.6-Sol is OpenAI's flagship tier, added to test whether it exceeds the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {above }\DIFdelendFL within the same generation; \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {its All-column bootstrap 95\% CI, $[0.731,0.806]$, overlaps Terra's $[0.736,0.810]$, so flagship does not separate from balanced despite leading on diffraction alone }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {the overall difference is $+0.002$ (95\% CI $[-0.042,0.046]$), not significant, so the design detects no difference between flagship and balanced tier larger than about 0.07} }\DIFaddendFL (Discussion). \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {Bold marks the column maximum; it does not imply a significant difference from the next model.}}\DIFaddendFL }{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Effect of Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, pooled across diffraction and spectra, $n=30$ items per (model, constraint-type) cell, $S=3$ seeds, $K=2$ rounds\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  , under the corrected spectrum oracle (Methods)}\DIFdelendFL  . $\Delta $: PCR gain (after minus before); $b$: regressions (before-consistent, after-inconsistent); $c$: fixes (before-inconsistent, after-consistent); $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on $(b,c)$; $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$: Holm-Bonferroni-adjusted $p$ over this table's 6-test family (Methods, Statistics paragraph\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  ; \texttt  {experiments/exp15\_multiplicity\_correction.py}}\DIFdelendFL  ). All four CGSV effects survive this \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  strictest }\DIFdelendFL  correction; both controls remain non-significant. \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}For CGSV, $b=0$ follows from the stopping rule (an item consistent before revision is never revised); the controls revise every item, so their $b$ is not comparable. Paired changes in correctness are in Table~\ref {tab:cgsvacc}.}}\DIFaddendFL  }}{16}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cgsv}{{3}{16}{\justifying Effect of Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, pooled across diffraction and spectra, $n=30$ items per (model, constraint-type) cell, $S=3$ seeds, $K=2$ rounds\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {, under the corrected spectrum oracle (Methods)}\DIFdelendFL . $\Delta $: PCR gain (after minus before); $b$: regressions (before-consistent, after-inconsistent); $c$: fixes (before-inconsistent, after-consistent); $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on $(b,c)$; $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$: Holm-Bonferroni-adjusted $p$ over this table's 6-test family (Methods, Statistics paragraph\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {; \texttt {experiments/exp15\_multiplicity\_correction.py}}\DIFdelendFL ). All four CGSV effects survive this \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {strictest }\DIFdelendFL correction; both controls remain non-significant. \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {For CGSV, $b=0$ follows from the stopping rule (an item consistent before revision is never revised); the controls revise every item, so their $b$ is not comparable. Paired changes in correctness are in Table~\ref {tab:cgsvacc}.}}\DIFaddendFL }{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \justifying  {\color {blue} \sf  Paired change in answer correctness before and after CGSV, on the same items as Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}. Accuracy uses the non-circular criteria of the accuracy-versus-consistency analysis (diffraction: within $0.1^\circ $ of the nearest reference reflection; spectra: within $2\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$ of the nearest detected local maximum), applied to each item's first-round and final-round claim (accuracy before$\to $after). L/G: items that stop (lost) or start (gained) being accurate; $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on (L, G). Rev.: items the oracle flagged at round 1 and the model revised; of these, A cleared the oracle with an accurate value, Ad cleared it with an admissible value outside the accuracy tolerance, and U were still flagged after $K=2$ rounds.}}}{17}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cgsvacc}{{4}{17}{\justifying \DIFaddFL {Paired change in answer correctness before and after CGSV, on the same items as Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}. Accuracy uses the non-circular criteria of the accuracy-versus-consistency analysis (diffraction: within $0.1^\circ $ of the nearest reference reflection; spectra: within $2\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$ of the nearest detected local maximum), applied to each item's first-round and final-round claim (accuracy before$\to $after). L/G: items that stop (lost) or start (gained) being accurate; $p$: exact two-sided McNemar test on (L, G). Rev.: items the oracle flagged at round 1 and the model revised; of these, A cleared the oracle with an accurate value, Ad cleared it with an admissible value outside the accuracy tolerance, and U were still flagged after $K=2$ rounds.}}{table.4}{}}
\citation{scirlvr2026}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \justifying  Verifier precision/recall/$F_1$ (in-distribution and held-out-constraint-type splits, both source-disjoint) and the detector's bootstrap 95\% CI on ROC AUC, predicting a real oracle violation from held-out verifier scores. Expected calibration error is reported in the text for the in-distribution split (ECE $=0.096$). No pooled across-constraint-type AUC is reported (see text).}}{18}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:verifier}{{5}{18}{\justifying Verifier precision/recall/$F_1$ (in-distribution and held-out-constraint-type splits, both source-disjoint) and the detector's bootstrap 95\% CI on ROC AUC, predicting a real oracle violation from held-out verifier scores. Expected calibration error is reported in the text for the in-distribution split (ECE $=0.096$). No pooled across-constraint-type AUC is reported (see text)}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {Physical consistency, oracle-driven correction, and violation detection.} \textbf  {a)} Physical-Consistency Rate (PCR) for nine models across the six label-free constraint types and overall, ordered by overall PCR; shading is linear in PCR\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  on the scale shown}\DIFdelendFL  . \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  Microscopy is the easiest constraint and Raman spectra the hardest and most model-dependent, from 0.068 (GPT-4o) to 0.833 (GPT-5.6-Terra). Within the DFT-grounded block the three types diverge for several models, which the pooled DFT column of Table~\ref {tab:main} conceals. Cell $n$ is 44--90 records; per-cell bootstrap intervals are in Supplementary Table~S1. }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}Stability uses the final oracle, which does not score near-hull materials. Cells hold 44--90 checkable records; intervals and coverage are in Supplementary Tables~S1 and S2.} }\DIFaddendFL  $^{\dagger }$Later-generation refresh models; $^{\ddagger }$flagship-tier addition\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  testing GPT-5.6-Sol against the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra}\DIFdelendFL  . \textbf  {b)} \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  PCR }\DIFaddendFL  before and after \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification }\DIFaddendFL  on identical items\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  (Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}). PCR rises for all four models with no regression in any cell}\DIFdelendFL  , \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  whereas }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  with }\DIFaddendFL  the two matched controls, \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  unaided }\DIFdelendFL  self-refinement and equal-compute re-prompting (grey, dashed) \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  , do not change PCR and do produce regressions, attributing the gain to the oracle signal rather than to additional inference-time compute}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}(Tables~\ref {tab:cgsv} and~\ref {tab:cgsvacc})}}\DIFaddendFL  . \textbf  {c)} Detector AUC with bootstrap 95\% \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  confidence }\DIFdelendFL  intervals \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  (Table~\ref {tab:verifier}) }\DIFdelendFL  for the released verifier, \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  its }\DIFdelendFL  two \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  comparator }\DIFdelendFL  baselines and the six held-out constraint types, on source-disjoint splits \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}(Table~\ref {tab:verifier})}}\DIFaddendFL  .\DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  In distribution the verifier separates violations; the LLM judge does not, and self-confidence falls \emph  {below} chance, so neither baseline substitutes for it. Held out, five of six types are not separable from chance, and metallicity's interval excludes chance nominally but not after correcting for the six comparisons.}\DIFdelendFL  }}{19}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:results}{{3}{19}{\justifying \textbf {Physical consistency, oracle-driven correction, and violation detection.} \textbf {a)} Physical-Consistency Rate (PCR) for nine models across the six label-free constraint types and overall, ordered by overall PCR; shading is linear in PCR\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {on the scale shown}\DIFdelendFL . \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {Microscopy is the easiest constraint and Raman spectra the hardest and most model-dependent, from 0.068 (GPT-4o) to 0.833 (GPT-5.6-Terra). Within the DFT-grounded block the three types diverge for several models, which the pooled DFT column of Table~\ref {tab:main} conceals. Cell $n$ is 44--90 records; per-cell bootstrap intervals are in Supplementary Table~S1. }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {Stability uses the final oracle, which does not score near-hull materials. Cells hold 44--90 checkable records; intervals and coverage are in Supplementary Tables~S1 and S2.} }\DIFaddendFL $^{\dagger }$Later-generation refresh models; $^{\ddagger }$flagship-tier addition\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {testing GPT-5.6-Sol against the balanced-tier GPT-5.6-Terra}\DIFdelendFL . \textbf {b)} \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {PCR }\DIFaddendFL before and after \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification }\DIFaddendFL on identical items\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {(Table~\ref {tab:cgsv}). PCR rises for all four models with no regression in any cell}\DIFdelendFL , \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {whereas }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {with }\DIFaddendFL the two matched controls, \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {unaided }\DIFdelendFL self-refinement and equal-compute re-prompting (grey, dashed) \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {, do not change PCR and do produce regressions, attributing the gain to the oracle signal rather than to additional inference-time compute}\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {(Tables~\ref {tab:cgsv} and~\ref {tab:cgsvacc})}}\DIFaddendFL . \textbf {c)} Detector AUC with bootstrap 95\% \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {confidence }\DIFdelendFL intervals \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {(Table~\ref {tab:verifier}) }\DIFdelendFL for the released verifier, \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {its }\DIFdelendFL two \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {comparator }\DIFdelendFL baselines and the six held-out constraint types, on source-disjoint splits \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {(Table~\ref {tab:verifier})}}\DIFaddendFL .\DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {In distribution the verifier separates violations; the LLM judge does not, and self-confidence falls \emph {below} chance, so neither baseline substitutes for it. Held out, five of six types are not separable from chance, and metallicity's interval excludes chance nominally but not after correcting for the six comparisons.}\DIFdelendFL }{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \justifying  {\color {blue} \sf  In-distribution verifier performance within each constraint family (the source-disjoint 80/20 test split of Table~\ref {tab:verifier}, broken down by family; scores are those of the single in-distribution model). AUC 95\% CI: bootstrap over source items ($B=5000$). Viol.: share of test records that are oracle violations. A baseline that scores each record by its family's training-set violation rate reaches a pooled AUC of 0.759, against the verifier's 0.850. Labels follow the original stability rule; on the items the final rule scores, the stability AUC is 0.911 (Supplementary Note~2).}}}{20}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:verifierfam}{{6}{20}{\justifying \DIFaddFL {In-distribution verifier performance within each constraint family (the source-disjoint 80/20 test split of Table~\ref {tab:verifier}, broken down by family; scores are those of the single in-distribution model). AUC 95\% CI: bootstrap over source items ($B=5000$). Viol.: share of test records that are oracle violations. A baseline that scores each record by its family's training-set violation rate reaches a pooled AUC of 0.759, against the verifier's 0.850. Labels follow the original stability rule; on the items the final rule scores, the stability AUC is 0.911 (Supplementary Note~2).}}{table.6}{}}
