\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \justifying  \textbf  {The MatPCR framework and what its metric adds to accuracy.} \textbf  {a)} Items and oracles are built from public characterization data and Materials Project references; the oracles are validated before any model is run, by a positive control that plants violations at three known rates and a human audit of oracle-fired cases; reasoning chains are then parsed into claims, checked, and summarized as the Physical-Consistency Rate with bootstrap intervals, whose displacement from the true rate Proposition 1 makes exact. The same oracle judgments drive Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which returns each violation to the model for revision, and supply the training labels for the released verifier. No step uses a human annotation of the correct answer. \textbf  {b)} A reasoning chain occupies one of four states. Accuracy resolves the rows and needs labels; PCR resolves the columns and does not. \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  The three constraint types with a non-circular accuracy criterion each populate a different off-diagonal cell: models are frequently close enough to satisfy the diffraction oracle while measurably off the exact peak, correct by Materials Project's own stability label yet outside the oracle's looser tolerance, and wrong on both counts for spectra. }\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}Models are frequently close enough to satisfy the diffraction oracle while measurably off the exact peak, and wrong on both counts for spectra; on stability the two axes coincide under the final rule, because the strict and operational definitions of stability differ only in the near-hull band, which is not scored.} }\DIFaddendFL  \textbf  {c)} The resulting disagreement, as the percentage of items on which the two axes differ, across the four originally evaluated models. \DIFdelbeginFL  {\color {red} \relax \fontsize  {9}{9}\selectfont  The mean is 22.2\% over the twelve (model, constraint-type) cells, so PCR is not answer accuracy under another name}\DIFdelendFL  \DIFaddbeginFL  {\color {blue} \sf  {\color  {blue}The mean is 23.6\% over the eight diffraction and spectra cells}}\DIFaddendFL  ; the spread across constraint types shows that the two axes come apart for different reasons in each.}}{28}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{5}{28}{\justifying \textbf {The MatPCR framework and what its metric adds to accuracy.} \textbf {a)} Items and oracles are built from public characterization data and Materials Project references; the oracles are validated before any model is run, by a positive control that plants violations at three known rates and a human audit of oracle-fired cases; reasoning chains are then parsed into claims, checked, and summarized as the Physical-Consistency Rate with bootstrap intervals, whose displacement from the true rate Proposition 1 makes exact. The same oracle judgments drive Constraint-Grounded Self-Verification, which returns each violation to the model for revision, and supply the training labels for the released verifier. No step uses a human annotation of the correct answer. \textbf {b)} A reasoning chain occupies one of four states. Accuracy resolves the rows and needs labels; PCR resolves the columns and does not. \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {The three constraint types with a non-circular accuracy criterion each populate a different off-diagonal cell: models are frequently close enough to satisfy the diffraction oracle while measurably off the exact peak, correct by Materials Project's own stability label yet outside the oracle's looser tolerance, and wrong on both counts for spectra. }\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {Models are frequently close enough to satisfy the diffraction oracle while measurably off the exact peak, and wrong on both counts for spectra; on stability the two axes coincide under the final rule, because the strict and operational definitions of stability differ only in the near-hull band, which is not scored.} }\DIFaddendFL \textbf {c)} The resulting disagreement, as the percentage of items on which the two axes differ, across the four originally evaluated models. \DIFdelbeginFL \DIFdelFL {The mean is 22.2\% over the twelve (model, constraint-type) cells, so PCR is not answer accuracy under another name}\DIFdelendFL \DIFaddbeginFL \DIFaddFL {\rev {The mean is 23.6\% over the eight diffraction and spectra cells}}\DIFaddendFL ; the spread across constraint types shows that the two axes come apart for different reasons in each}{figure.5}{}}
